BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Unca": "https://www.unca.edu/event/troublesome-rising-writing-when-the-water-recedes/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:unca
X-SOURCE:UNC Asheville
DTSTART;TZID=America/Halifax:20270225T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Halifax:20270225T190000
DTSTAMP:20261123T202918Z
CREATED:20261123T202321Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261123T202918Z
UID:10000164-1803576600-1803582000@www.unca.edu
SUMMARY:Troublesome Rising: Writing When the Water Recedes
DESCRIPTION:In Troublesome Rising: Writing When the Water Recedes\, authors Melissa Helton\, Robert Gipe\, and Mandi Fugate Sheffel reflect on their experiences during the historic 2022 flood in Hindman\, Kentucky\, and their contributions to Troublesome Rising: A Thousand-Year Flood in Eastern Kentucky. Through individual presentations and a panel discussion\, they will explore how creative writing can represent unthinkable loss\, document the experiences of Appalachian communities\, and leave room for a hopeful tomorrow. \nThis event will take place in the Blue Ridge Room of Highsmith Student Union and is free and open to the public; however\, registration is encouraged. Register Here \n“Troublesome Rising: Writing When the Water Recedes” is the fourth event in the “Rising Waters: Writing Place and Environment” Thomas Howerton Professorship lecture series presented by the UNC Asheville English Department. This three-year series features humanities scholars in conversation with natural sciences scholars about issues affecting us all. \nMelissa Helton \nMelissa Jørgenrud Helton is Literary Arts Director of Hindman Settlement School\, a cultural nonprofit in Knott County\, Kentucky. Her work has been published in Shenandoah\, Women Speak\, Still: The Journal\, Anthology of Appalachian Writers\, Norwegian Writers Climate Campaign\, and more. Her chapbooks include Inertia: A Study and Hewn\, and her first full length collection is A Middle-Aged Woman Rages. \nShe is editor of the anthology Troublesome Rising: A Thousand-Year Flood in Eastern Kentucky which documents the deadly flood of 2022\, and the literary and arts magazine Untelling. She is series editor for the Hindman Seedbed series under the Swallow imprint of Ohio University Press. Her work has been supported through the Kentucky Foundation for Women\, and has been awarded the George Scarbrough Poetry Prize\, the Emma Bell Miles Nonfiction Prize\, has been nominated for Best of the Net\, and has been a finalist for multiple awards including the Weatherford Award. \nOriginally from Toledo\, Ohio\, she has called southeast Kentucky home since 2010. She was an educator for various colleges and universities for 17 years\, and is a member of the Kentucky Poetry Society and Appalachian Studies Association. She has been honored with the Mildred Haun Award of Excellence and designation as a Kentucky Colonel for her work after the 2022 flood\, and she is a dual citizen in the United Kingdom. \nMandi Fugate Sheffel \nMandi Fugate Sheffel was born and raised in Red Fox\, KY. She graduated from Eastern Kentucky University and currently serves as the Sycamore Fund Project Coordinator at the Foundation for Appalachian Kentucky. She is vice chair of the Mountain Association board. Additionally\, she owns and operates Read Spotted Newt\, an independent bookstore in the coalfields of eastern Kentucky. Her personal essays and opinion pieces can be found in Still: The Journal\, Lexington-Herald Leader\, Salon\, and the Courier Journal. Her personal essay collection\, The Nature of Pain\, is available now through The University Press of Kentucky. \nRobert Gipe \nRobert Gipe won the 2015 Weatherford Award for outstanding Appalachian novel for his first novel Trampoline. His second novel\, Weedeater\,  was published in 2018. His third novel\, Pop\, ​was published in 2021. All three novels are published by Ohio University Press. In 2021\, the trilogy won the Judy Gaines Young Book Award. From 1997 to 2018\, Gipe directed the Southeast Kentucky Community & Technical College Appalachian Program in Harlan. Gipe is founding producer of the Higher Ground community performance series\, and has served as a script consultant for the Hulu series Dopesick and a producer on the feature film The Evening Hour. Gipe resides in Harlan County\, Kentucky. He grew up in Kingsport\, Tennessee.​ \nAccessibility\nUNC Asheville is committed to providing universal access to all of our events. If you have any questions about access or would like to request reasonable accommodations that will facilitate your full participation in the film screenings\, please contact Stephanie Bullick\, Director of Accessibility\, at accessibility@unca.edu. \nVisitor Parking\n\nAll visitors must adhere to the University’s parking policy. Please visit the UNC Asheville Visitor Parking Permit Registration site to acquire a visitor permit to park on campus.
URL:https://www.unca.edu/event/troublesome-rising-writing-when-the-water-recedes/
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://www.unca.edu/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Event_Calendar_-_TEMPLATE_77.png
END:VEVENT
